  "mode": "major",
  "key": "F major (Malin's frame)",
  "tonic": "D4",
  "degrees": {
    "frame": "Malin 2016's frame, F = 1 (torah-grammar.md L57). TropeTrainer files this table as \"C MAJOR\" because it calls the low sof-pasuk note (5_) the tonic; the pitches are the same. Binder's 'sof pasuk ends on C' reads here as 1, 5_.",
    "numbering": "Diatonic steps of the major scale from 1. 8 = octave. 0 is the 7th below (the report's 7_), -1 the 6th below (6_), -2 the 5th below (5_). alt: +1 raises a note a semitone (the report's 4# = B natural, and Common Melody's 2#).",
    "semitones": {
      "0": -1,
      "1": 0,
      "2": 2,
      "3": 4,
      "4": 5,
      "5": 7,
      "6": 9,
      "7": 11,
      "8": 12,
      "-2": -5,
      "-1": -3
    },
    "keyPins": "Degree 1. scoreVerse's key option (key: \"D4\") names the pitch of degree 1, as with the haftarah table, so key: \"F3\" reproduces the published pitches (the silluq ends on C3) and the default \"D4\" puts the sof pasuk's low 5_ on A3 and the shalshelet's top 6 on B4, an octave and a second (Rosowsky's 8 reaches D5), inside a baritone's chant range. Pinning 1 rather than TropeTrainer's C keeps one meaning of key across both tables (the mode's reciting tone) and keeps the degrees aligned with the literature."
  },
  "durations": {
    "unit": "beats (quarter note = 1)",
    "default": 0.5,
    "held": 1.5,
    "note": "The report gives rhythm only as TropeTrainer's held mark ('). Held = 1.5, everything else the engine's default eighth note; rhythm is free (trope-grammar.md §2.2 rule 6)."
  },
